WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.140
Hey Crazies
In a previous video, I said this:

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.040
It's physically impossible for light not to go at the speed of light.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.260
and a bunch of you got all defensive about it.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.540
So, today, we’re finally going to answer
the question.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.880
Can light slow down?

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.520
Well, that depends.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.420
Say you wanted to send a laser through something like this.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.000
It’s just a piece of glass,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.960
nothing special,
but something weird happens when this laser

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.109
passes through it.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:28.560
If we measure the distance the light has to
travel

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.940
and the time it takes to travel that distance
OK, so we’re not measuring that time with

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.040
a stop watch,
but we can measure it if we have the

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:36.880
proper tools.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:37.920
Just go with me here.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.380
Dividing the distance by the time gives us
a speed less than the speed of light.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.680
If you’re quick to judgement, you might
be like:

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.800
See! It does slow down!”

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.580
But we haven’t necessarily shown that.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.140
There’s a problem with the way we calculated
that number.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.980
Dividing distance by time doesn’t give you
a speed.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.440
It gives you an average speed.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.320
And you all remember what I said about averages
in the last video.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.940
Sometimes they have meaning and sometimes they don't.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.420
Meaning is what we're looking for in science.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.140
We need to figure out if that average speed

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.740
has meaning,
so let’s take another look at that light.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.820
It almost looks like it has drag on it.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.220
Kinda like a person trying to run through
mud.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.940
Except drag only happens when a solid tries to pass through a fluid.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.289
Light is not solid and this glass prism is
not a fluid.

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:24.960
A common explanation is that the light ceases
to exist for a moment

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.380
as it’s absorbed and released by the atoms
in the glass.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.450
It’s a nice simple explanation for beginners, but it's wrong.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.929
We’ve already seen that electrons in glass
can’t absorb

00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:36.160
any wavelengths of visible light to jump to
higher energy levels.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.920
That’s why it’s transparent.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.940
Another common explanation is that the light
doesn’t travel in a straight line.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.340
It keeps bouncing off atoms along the way.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.480
Also wrong!

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.020
If that were the case, different parts of
the beam

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.780
would come out of different parts of the glass,
randomly.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.280
But we know the beam is very controlled and
predictable.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.300
Something else must be going on here.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.460
We need to stop thinking of light as if it’s
a stream of bullets.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.700
Even if you imagine the light is made of photons,
those photons are not bullets.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.140
Light is a wave, which means we need to worry
about wave interference.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.600
When you have two or more waves in the same
spot, they combine to form one wave.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.100
Isn’t the original beam only one light wave
though?

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.960
Well, technically, it’s a bunch of waves moving together

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.780
plus the original beam isn’t the only light
in that glass.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.630
The moment the beam comes into contact with
the glass,

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.960
its atoms start to vibrate in response.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.720
That vibration causes all those atoms to emit
their own light.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.850
Most of the light cancels except for one particular
direction:

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.380
the forward direction.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.140
The point is, when you combine all those waves
together

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.760
they just look like one beam traveling slower than the speed of light.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.140
But is the light actually traveling slower?

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.760
That depends on what you mean by “light.”

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.200
It’s a language issue.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.850
I think everyone agrees that one single photon
is “light”

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.710
but this beam has a bunch of photons in it.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:01.060
If you also refer to that beam as “light”
then, sure, “light” slows down in materials.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.320
But, when I said:

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.440
It's physically impossible for light not to go at the speed of light.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:11.520
I was referring to individual mass-less photons,
which always go the same speed.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.060
Beams of light don’t always have the same
properties as their photons.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.820
So would you refer to that beam as “light”
or a bunch of “lights”?

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.200
Let us know down in the comments.

